  "notes": "Teen pop-rock — a skate-punk surface on Matrix-written pop structures — modelled on the mid-tempo band the sourced single sits in: 80–100 straight with the backbeat on 2&4. The 150–175 pop-punk band is the other half of the catalogue and is named here rather than encoded, because a single tempo window cannot carry both and the documented single is the mid-tempo. Harmony comes from the writing method rather than from theory: the writers \"start from 'basic chords' played on acoustic guitar and build the topline over them\", giving I–V–vi–IV and vi–IV–I–V in guitar-friendly majors with one key per song and no modulation. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"kick 1 and the '&' of 3\", \"snare 2&4 with a rimshot ghost\" (a real ghost probability with a rim lane), and \"tambourine on the chorus backbeat\"; the kit is live with tight editing, so the grid stays hard while the velocity spreads. The bass is \"played bass on roots, 8th notes, doubling the guitar; simple root-fifth motion\" — `mirror_kick` at 0.95 with the fifth as its only passing tone. The melody is \"speech-like and low in the verse with a sneered attack, chorus a stepwise climb to a held note\", which is the low register, the high step share and the arch contour. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability and the humanize values; the entry names placements and instruments, not numbers.",
